	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/plain" rect="(20.0, 20.0, 900.0, 86.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">PRAKTIKUM 5 — Features and PCA

PART A (top): try things with FEATURES and let Test and Score judge them -- same learners, same folds, only the columns change.
PART B (bottom): PCA REQUIRES SCALING. The two PCA branches differ in one thing only.</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/plain" rect="(1080.0, 120.0, 380.0, 440.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">PART A — WHAT TO DO
1. Compare the two Rank widgets. Where do dti,
   pti and util land once they exist? Where were
   debt and income before?
2. Box Plot: does dti look different for defaulters?
   Switch the variable to debt, then income.
3. Open the three Test and Score widgets, sort by
   AUC, and fill in a 4 x 3 table. Which model
   gains most from the ratios? Which gains least?
4. Unscaled kNN scores EXACTLY the same with and
   without the ratios; scaled kNN gains the most.
   Why can unscaled kNN not see dti, pti, util?
5. 'Ratios only' has 3 features instead of 16.
   Unscaled kNN suddenly jumps. What did removing
   the rupiah columns do to its distances?
6. Try your own: add a 4th expression in Feature
   Constructor (e.g. loan_amount / max(income, 1))
   and watch all three Test and Score update.
7. Continuize: switch the treatment of categorical
   columns (one-hot, ordinal, remove) and look at
   the Encoded data table.</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/plain" rect="(820.0, 640.0, 380.0, 250.0)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="13">PART B — WHAT TO DO
1. Preprocess: confirm 'Normalize Features' is ON.
   In both PCA widgets, 'Normalize variables' is
   deliberately OFF, so Preprocess is the only
   difference between the two branches.
2. Open BOTH PCA widgets. How many components
   reach 95% variance in each? (Unscaled: very
   few. Scaled: many more.) Why?
3. Compare the two scatter plots. The unscaled one
   is essentially a plot of one variable. Which?

See book section 5.6.4, limitation 2.</text>
	</annotations>
